mment":""},{"DocComment":" This overwrites any previous value without dropping it, so be careful"},{"DocComment":" not to use this twice unless you want to skip running the destructor."},{"DocComment":" For your convenience, this also returns a mutable reference to the"},{"DocComment":" (now safely initialized) contents of `self`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" As the content is stored inside a `ManuallyDrop`, the destructor is not"},{"DocComment":" run for the inner data if the MaybeUninit leaves scope without a call to"},{"DocComment":" [`assume_init`], [`assume_init_drop`], or similar. Code that receives"},{"DocComment":" the mutable reference returned by this function needs to keep this in"},{"DocComment":" mind. The safety model of Rust regards leaks as safe, but they are"},{"DocComment":" usually still undesirable. This being said, the mutable reference"},{"DocComment":" behaves like any other mutable reference would, so assigning a new value"},{"DocComment":" to it will drop the old content."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`assume_init`]: Self::assume_init"},{"DocComment":" [`assume_init_drop`]: Self::assume_init_drop"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Correct usage of this method:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" use std::mem::MaybeUninit;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut x = MaybeUninit::<Vec<u8>>::uninit();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" {"},{"DocComment":"     let hello = x.write((&b\"Hello, world!\").to_vec());"},{"DocComment":"     // Setting hello does not leak prior allocations, but drops them"},{"DocComment":"     *hello = (&b\"Hello\").to_vec();"},{"DocComment":"     hello[0] = 'h' as u8;"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" // x is initialized now:"},{"DocComment":" let s = unsafe { x.assume_init() };"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(b\"hello\", s.as_slice());"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This usage of the method causes a